## Our Takeaway

The [executive order](https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/10/decreasing-housing-costs-for-the-american-people/) does not cancel private mortgage insurance (PMI) automatically for anyone today. Section 2 only permits the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) Director to establish a pilot program for future Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac mortgages. Existing borrowers receive no coverage under this text, and buyers who put 20 percent down carry no insurance to cancel. The order names no specific equity percentage or start date. Our internal modeling of the PMI timeline, calculated using scenario inputs instead of a product test, shows that earlier cancellation depends entirely on property appreciation and the rules the agency sets. This outlook changes only if the FHFA publishes formal pilot terms with an active start date and an equity threshold.

Borrowers must continue using standard cancellation paths established under federal law. Check your loan statement for the 80 percent date against [CFPB cancellation rules](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/when-can-i-remove-private-mortgage-insurance-pmi-from-my-loan-en-202/), then submit a cancellation request to your servicer in writing. Making additional principal payments helps reach that 80 percent mark earlier, which you can track using our [mortgage extra payment calculator](/mortgage/extra-payment-calculator/). Servicers must terminate coverage automatically once the balance falls to 78 percent of original value if your account remains current.